<div class="thumbcaption" id="mwBkM">Flowchart of the life cycle of a Debian package</div></div></div>

<p id="mwBkQ">Each software package has a <i id="mwBkU">maintainer</i> that may be either one person or a team of Debian developers and non-developer maintainers.<sup about="#mwt734" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-171" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-171"}}'><a href="#cite_note-171" id="mwBkY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBkc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBkg">[</span>171<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBkk">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt737" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-172" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-172"}}'><a href="#cite_note-172" id="mwBko"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBks"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBkw">[</span>172<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBk0">]</span></span></a></sup> The maintainer keeps track of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upstream_(software_development)" title="Upstream (software development)" id="mwBk4">upstream</a> releases, and ensures that the package coheres with the rest of the distribution and meets the standards of quality of Debian. Packages may include modifications introduced by Debian to achieve compliance with Debian Policy, even to fix non-Debian specific bugs, although coordination with upstream developers is advised.<sup about="#mwt740" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-173" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-173"}}'><a href="#cite_note-173" id="mwBk8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBlA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBlE">[</span>173<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBlI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBlM">The maintainer releases a new version by uploading the package to the "incoming" system, which verifies the integrity of the packages and their <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_signature" title="Digital signature" id="mwBlQ">digital signatures</a>. If the package is found to be valid, it is installed in the package archive into an area called the <i id="mwBlU">pool</i> and distributed every day to hundreds of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_mirror" title="Web mirror" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBlY">mirrors</a> worldwide. As of April 5, 2025, there were a total of 379 Debian mirrors operating.<sup about="#mwt741" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-debian-mirrors-list_93-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"debian-mirrors-list"}}'><a href="#cite_note-debian-mirrors-list-93" id="mwBlc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBlg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBlk">[</span>93<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBlo">]</span></span></a></sup> The upload must be signed using <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenPGP" title="OpenPGP" id="mwBls">OpenPGP</a>-compatible software. All Debian developers have individual <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public-key_cryptography" title="Public-key cryptography" id="mwBlw">cryptographic key pairs</a>.<sup about="#mwt744" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-174" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-174"}}'><a href="#cite_note-174" id="mwBl0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBl4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBl8">[</span>174<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBmA">]</span></span></a></sup> Developers are responsible for any package they upload even if the packaging was prepared by another contributor.<sup about="#mwt747" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-175" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-175"}}'><a href="#cite_note-175" id="mwBmE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBmI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBmM">[</span>175<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBmQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBmU">Initially, an accepted package is only available in the <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt748" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBmY" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"unstable"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt748" id="mwBmc">unstable</span> branch. For a package to become a candidate for the next release, it must migrate to the <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt750" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBmg" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"testing"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt750" id="mwBmk">testing</span> branch by meeting the following:<sup about="#mwt754" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-176" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-176"}}'><a href="#cite_note-176" id="mwBmo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBms"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBmw">[</span>176<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBm0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<ul id="mwBm4"><li id="mwBm8">It has been in <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt755" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBnA" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"unstable"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt755" id="mwBnE">unstable</span> for a certain length of time that depends on the urgency of the changes.</li>
<li id="mwBnI">It does not have "release-critical" bugs, except for the ones already present in <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt757" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBnM" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"testing"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt757" id="mwBnQ">testing</span>. Release-critical bugs are those considered serious enough that they make the package unsuitable for release.</li>
<li id="mwBnU">There are no outdated versions in <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt759" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBnY" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"unstable"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt759" id="mwBnc">unstable</span> for any release ports.</li>
<li id="mwBng">The migration does not break any packages in <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt761" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBnk" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"testing"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt761" id="mwBno">testing</span>.</li>
<li id="mwBns">Its dependencies can be satisfied by packages already in <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt763" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBnw" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"testing"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt763" id="mwBn0">testing</span> or by packages being migrated at the same time.</li>
<li id="mwBn4">The migration is not blocked by a freeze.</li></ul>

<p id="mwBn8">Thus, a release-critical bug in a new version of a shared library on which many packages depend may prevent those packages from entering <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt765" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBoA" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"testing"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt765" id="mwBoE">testing</span>, because the updated library must meet the requirements too.<sup about="#mwt771" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-177" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-177"}}'><a href="#cite_note-177" id="mwBoI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBoM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBoQ">[</span>177<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBoU">]</span></span></a></sup> From the branch viewpoint, the migration process happens twice per day, rendering <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt767" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBoY" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"testing"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt767" id="mwBoc">testing</span> in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perpetual_beta" title="Perpetual beta" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBog">perpetual beta</a>.</p>

<p id="mwBok">Periodically, the release team publishes guidelines to the developers in order to ready the release. A new release occurs after a freeze, when all important software is reasonably up-to-date in the <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt772" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBoo" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"testing"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt772" id="mwBos">testing</span> branch and any other significant issues are solved. At that time, all packages in the <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt774" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBow" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"testing"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt774" id="mwBo0">testing</span> branch become the new <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt776" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBo4" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"stable"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt776" id="mwBo8">stable</span> branch. Although freeze dates are time-based,<sup about="#mwt778" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-two-year-cycle_66-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"two-year-cycle"}}'><a href="#cite_note-two-year-cycle-66" id="mwBpA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBpE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBpI">[</span>66<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBpM">]</span></span></a></sup> release dates are not, which are announced by the release managers a couple of weeks beforehand.<sup about="#mwt781" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-178" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-178"}}'><a href="#cite_note-178" id="mwBpQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBpU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBpY">[</span>178<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBpc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBpg">A version of a package can belong to more than one branch, usually <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt782" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBpk" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"testing"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt782" id="mwBpo">testing</span> and <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt784" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBps" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"unstable"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt784" id="mwBpw">unstable</span>. It is possible for a package to keep the same version between stable releases and be part of <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt786" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBp0" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"oldstable"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt786" id="mwBp4">oldstable</span>, <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt788" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBp8" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"stable"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt788" id="mwBqA">stable</span>, <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt790" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBqE" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"testing"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt790" id="mwBqI">testing</span> and <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt792" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBqM" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"unstable"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt792" id="mwBqQ">unstable</span> at the same time.<sup about="#mwt796" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-179" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-179"}}'><a href="#cite_note-179" id="mwBqU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBqY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBqc">[</span>179<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBqg">]</span></span></a></sup> Each branch can be seen as a collection of pointers into the package "pool" mentioned above.<sup about="#mwt797" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-distributions_115-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"distributions"}}'><a href="#cite_note-distributions-115" id="mwBqk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBqo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBqs">[</span>115<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBqw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBq0">One way to resolve the challenge of a release-critical bug in a new application version is the use of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="#Cross-distribution_package_manager" class="mw-selflink-fragment" id="mwBq4">optional package managers</a>. They allow software developers to use sandbox environments, while at the same time remaining in control of security.<sup about="#mwt800" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-180" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-180"}}'><a href="#cite_note-180" id="mwBq8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBrA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBrE">[</span>180<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBrI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt803" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-181" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-181"}}'><a href="#cite_note-181" id="mwBrM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBrQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBrU">[</span>181<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBrY">]</span></span></a></sup> Another benefit of a cross-distribution package manager is that they allow application developers to directly provide updates to users without going through distributions, and without having to package and test the application separately for each distribution.<sup about="#mwt806" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-182" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-182"}}'><a href="#cite_note-182" id="mwBrc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBrg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBrk">[</span>182<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBro">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="50" id="mwBrs" aria-labelledby="Release_cycle"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Release_cycle">Release cycle</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Debian&amp;action=edit&amp;section=50" title="Edit section: Release cycle"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwBrw">A new <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt807" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBr0" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"stable"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt807" id="mwBr4">stable</span> branch of Debian is released about every 2 years. It will receive official support for about 3 years with update for major security or usability fixes. Point releases will be available every several months as determined by Stable Release Managers (SRM).<sup about="#mwt811" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-183" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-183"}}'><a href="#cite_note-183" id="mwBr8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBsA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBsE">[</span>183<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBsI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBsM">Debian also launched its Long Term Support (LTS) project since Debian 6 "Squeeze". For each Debian release, it will receive two years of extra security updates provided by LTS Team after its End Of Life (EOL). However, no point releases will be made. Now each Debian release can receive 5 years of security support in total.<sup about="#mwt814" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-184" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-184"}}'><a href="#cite_note-184" id="mwBsQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBsU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBsY">[</span>184<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBsc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="51" id="mwBsg" aria-labelledby="Security"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Security">Security</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Debian&amp;action=edit&amp;section=51" title="Edit section: Security"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwBsk">The Debian project handles security through <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Full_disclosure_(computer_security)" title="Full disclosure (computer security)" id="mwBso">public disclosure</a>. Debian security advisories are compatible with the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Vulnerabilities_and_Exposures" title="Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures" id="mwBss">Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures</a> dictionary, are usually coordinated with other free software vendors and are published the same day a vulnerability is made public.<sup about="#mwt817" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-185" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-185"}}'><a href="#cite_note-185" id="mwBsw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBs0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBs4">[</span>185<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBs8">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt820" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-186" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-186"}}'><a href="#cite_note-186" id="mwBtA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBtE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBtI">[</span>186<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBtM">]</span></span></a></sup> There used to be a security audit project that focused on packages in the stable release looking for security bugs;<sup about="#mwt823" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-187" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-187"}}'><a href="#cite_note-187" id="mwBtQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBtU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBtY">[</span>187<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBtc">]</span></span></a></sup> Steve Kemp, who started the project, retired in 2011 but resumed his activities and applied to rejoin in 2014.<sup about="#mwt826" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-188" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-188"}}'><a href="#cite_note-188" id="mwBtg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBtk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBto">[</span>188<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBts">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBtw">The <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt827" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBt0" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"stable"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt827" id="mwBt4">stable</span> branch is supported by the Debian security team; <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt829" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBt8" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"oldstable"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt829" id="mwBuA">oldstable</span> is supported for one year. Although Squeeze was not officially supported, Debian coordinated an effort to provide <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long-term_support" title="Long-term support" id="mwBuE">long-term support</a> for IA-32 and x86-64 platforms, until February 2016, five years after the initial release.<sup about="#mwt842" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-189" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-189"}}'><a href="#cite_note-189" id="mwBuI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBuM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBuQ">[</span>189<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBuU">]</span></span></a></sup> <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt831" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBuY" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"testing"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt831" id="mwBuc">testing</span> is supported by the <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt833" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBug" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"testing"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt833" id="mwBuk">testing</span> security team, but does not receive updates in as timely a manner as <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt835" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBuo" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"stable"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt835" id="mwBus">stable</span>.<sup about="#mwt845" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-190" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-190"}}'><a href="#cite_note-190" id="mwBuw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBu0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBu4">[</span>190<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBu8">]</span></span></a></sup> <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt837" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwBvA" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"unstable"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt837" id="mwBvE">unstable</span><span class="nowrap" style="padding-left:0.1em;" about="#mwt839" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBvI" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"&apos;","href":"./Template:&apos;"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}'><span typeof="mw:Entity">'</span></span>s security is left for the package maintainers.<sup about="#mwt846" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-security-faq_114-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"security-faq"}}'><a href="#cite_note-security-faq-114" id="mwBvM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBvQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBvU">[</span>114<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBvY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBvc">The Debian project offers documentation and tools to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hardening_(computing)" title="Hardening (computing)" id="mwBvg">harden</a> a Debian installation both manually and automatically.<sup about="#mwt849" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-191" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-191"}}'><a href="#cite_note-191" id="mwBvk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBvo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBvs">[</span>191<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBvw">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AppArmor" title="AppArmor" id="mwBv0">AppArmor</a> support is available and enabled by default since Buster.<sup about="#mwt850" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-buster-released_83-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"buster-released"}}'><a href="#cite_note-buster-released-83" id="mwBv4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBv8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBwA">[</span>83<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBwE">]</span></span></a></sup> Debian provides an optional hardening wrapper, and does not harden all of its software by default using <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU_Compiler_Collection" title="GNU Compiler Collection" id="mwBwI">gcc</a> features such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Position-independent_code" title="Position-independent code" id="mwBwM">PIE</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buffer_overflow_protection" title="Buffer overflow protection" id="mwBwQ">buffer overflow protection</a>, unlike operating systems such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenBSD" title="OpenBSD" id="mwBwU">OpenBSD</a>,<sup about="#mwt853" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-192" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-192"}}'><a href="#cite_note-192" id="mwBwY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBwc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBwg">[</span>192<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBwk">]</span></span></a></sup> but tries to build as many packages as possible with hardening flags.<sup about="#mwt856" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-193" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-193"}}'><a href="#cite_note-193" id="mwBwo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBws"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBww">[</span>193<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBw0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="52" id="mwBw4" aria-labelledby="Branding"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Branding">Branding</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Debian&amp;action=edit&amp;section=52" title="Edit section: Branding"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBw8"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Debian-OpenLogo.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBxA"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Debian-OpenLogo.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Debian-OpenLogo.svg/120px-Debian-OpenLogo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/Debian-OpenLogo.svg/250px-Debian-OpenLogo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="Debian logo with text: debian" data-file-width="109" data-file-height="144" data-file-type="drawing" height="145" width="110" class="mw-file-element" id="mwBxE"/></a><figcaption id="mwBxI">The official logo (also known as open use logo) that contains the well-known Debian swirl</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwBxM">Debian has two logos. The official logo (also known as <i id="mwBxQ">open use logo</i>) contains the well-known Debian <i id="mwBxU">swirl</i> and is the visual identity of the Debian Project. A separate logo also exists for use by the Debian Project and its members only.<sup about="#mwt859" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-194" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-194"}}'><a href="#cite_note-194" id="mwBxY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBxc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBxg">[</span>194<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBxk">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBxo">The Debian "swirl" logo was designed by Raul Silva<sup about="#mwt863" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-195" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-195"}}'><a href="#cite_note-195" id="mwBxs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBxw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBx0">[</span>195<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBx4">]</span></span></a></sup> in 1999 as part of a contest to replace the semi-official logo that had been used.<sup about="#mwt866" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-196" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-196"}}'><a href="#cite_note-196" id="mwBx8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwByA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwByE">[</span>196<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwByI">]</span></span></a></sup> The winner of the contest received an @Debian.org <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Email_address" title="Email address" id="mwByM">email address</a>, and a set of Debian 2.1 install CDs for the architecture of their choice. Initially, the swirl was magic smoke arising from an also included bottle of an Arabian-style genie presented in black profile, but shortly after was reduced to the red smoke swirl for situations where space or multiple colours were not an option, and before long the bottle version effectively was superseded.<link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:All_accuracy_disputes" about="#mwt860" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwByQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Disputed inline","href":"./Template:Disputed_inline"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Debian Logo"},"date":{"wt":"July 2024"}},"i":0}}]}'/><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_with_disputed_statements_from_July_2024" about="#mwt860"/><sup class="noprint Inline-Template " style="white-space:nowrap;" about="#mwt860" id="mwByU"><span typeof="mw:Entity">[</span><i><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Disputed_statement" title="Wikipedia:Disputed statement" class="mw-redirect"><span title="This claim has reliable sources with contradicting facts (July 2024)">disputed</span></a><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span><span typeof="mw:Entity">–</span> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Debian#Debian_Logo" title="Talk:Debian">discuss</a></i><span typeof="mw:Entity">]</span></sup> There has been no official statement from the Debian project on the logo's meaning, but at the time of the logo's selection, it was suggested that the logo represented the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magic_smoke" title="Magic smoke" id="mwByY">magic smoke</a> that made computers work.<sup about="#mwt869" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-197" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-197"}}'><a href="#cite_note-197" id="mwByc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwByg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwByk">[</span>197<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwByo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt872" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-198" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-198"}}'><a href="#cite_note-198" id="mwBys"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwByw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBy0">[</span>198<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBy4">]</span></span></a></sup>
<sup about="#mwt875" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-199" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-199"}}'><a href="#cite_note-199" id="mwBy8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBzA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBzE">[</span>199<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBzI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBzM">One theory about the origin of the Debian logo is that <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buzz_Lightyear" title="Buzz Lightyear" id="mwBzQ">Buzz Lightyear</a>, the chosen character for the first named Debian release, has a swirl in his chin.<sup about="#mwt876" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKrafft200566_200-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEKrafft200566"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEKrafft200566-200"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Krafft"},"2":{"wt":"2005"},"p":{"wt":"66"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKrafft200566-200" id="mwBzU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBzY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBzc">[</span>200<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBzg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt881" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-201" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-201"}}'><a href="#cite_note-201" id="mwBzk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBzo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBzs">[</span>201<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBzw">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stefano_Zacchiroli" title="Stefano Zacchiroli" id="mwBz0">Stefano Zacchiroli</a> also suggested that this swirl is the Debian one.<sup about="#mwt884" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-202" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-202"}}'><a href="#cite_note-202" id="mwBz4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBz8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB0A">[</span>202<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB0E">]</span></span></a></sup> Buzz Lightyear's swirl is a more likely candidate as the codenames for Debian are names of Toy Story characters. The former Debian project leader <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bruce_Perens" title="Bruce Perens" id="mwB0I">Bruce Perens</a> used to work for Pixar and is credited as a studio tools engineer on <i id="mwB0M"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toy_Story_2" title="Toy Story 2" id="mwB0Q">Toy Story 2</a></i> (1999).</p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="53" id="mwB0U" aria-labelledby="Institutional_users"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Institutional_users">Institutional users</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Debian&amp;action=edit&amp;section=53" title="Edit section: Institutional users"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwB0Y">Debian is used by several institutions, such as many universities, NGOs and other non-profit organizations (including the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikimedia_Foundation" title="Wikimedia Foundation" id="mwB0c">Wikimedia Foundation</a>),<sup about="#mwt887" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-203" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-203"}}'><a href="#cite_note-203" id="mwB0g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB0k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB0o">[</span>203<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB0s">]</span></span></a></sup> and commercial companies.<sup about="#mwt890" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-204" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-204"}}'><a href="#cite_note-204" id="mwB0w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB00"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB04">[</span>204<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB08">]</span></span></a></sup> It has even been used in space, in laptops on board the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Space_Station" title="International Space Station" id="mwB1A">International Space Station</a>.<sup about="#mwt893" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-205" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-205"}}'><a href="#cite_note-205" id="mwB1E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB1I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB1M">[</span>205<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB1Q">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwB1U">Debian has been very helpful to numerous government agencies in the public sector, such as in the city of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munich" title="Munich" id="mwB1Y">Munich</a>, which used a Debian-based distribution in its <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LiMux" title="LiMux" id="mwB1c">LiMux</a> initiative for the government computer migration to Linux.<sup about="#mwt896" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-206" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-206"}}'><a href="#cite_note-206" id="mwB1g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB1k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB1o">[</span>206<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB1s">]</span></span></a></sup> Schools in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extremadura" title="Extremadura" id="mwB1w">Extremadura</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andalusia" title="Andalusia" id="mwB10">Andalusia</a> (Spain) also utilized Debian-based systems (gnuLinEx and Guadalinex, respectively) to develop digital skills and open-source computing in schools.<sup about="#mwt899" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-207" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-207"}}'><a href="#cite_note-207" id="mwB14"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB18"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB2A">[</span>207<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB2E">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt902" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-208" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-208"}}'><a href="#cite_note-208" id="mwB2I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB2M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB2Q">[</span>208<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB2U">]</span></span></a></sup> There are many other cases of usage of Debian-based distributions in education, such as the deployment of Skolelinux/Debian Edu in Norwegian schools.<sup about="#mwt905" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-209" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-209"}}'><a href="#cite_note-209" id="mwB2Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB2c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB2g">[</span>209<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB2k">]</span></span></a></sup> In addition, other public administrations use Linux systems indirectly based on Debian, such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Gendarmerie" title="French Gendarmerie" class="mw-redirect" id="mwB2o">French Gendarmerie</a>, which uses Ubuntu-derived <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GendBuntu" title="GendBuntu" id="mwB2s">GendBuntu</a> distribution.<sup about="#mwt908" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-210" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-210"}}'><a href="#cite_note-210" id="mwB2w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB20"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB24">[</span>210<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB28">]</span></span></a></sup>The Five Distros That Changed Linux</p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="54" id="mwB3A" aria-labelledby="Derivatives"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="Derivatives">Derivatives</h2><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Debian&amp;action=edit&amp;section=54" title="Edit section: Derivatives"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt909" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwB3E" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Further","href":"./Template:Further"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"List of Linux distributions#Debian-based"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt910" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt909" id="mwB3I">Further information: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Linux_distributions#Debian-based" title="List of Linux distributions">List of Linux distributions §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Debian-based</a></div>
<p id="mwB3M">Debian is one of the most popular Linux distributions, and many other distributions have been created from the Debian codebase.<sup about="#mwt917" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-211" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-211"}}'><a href="#cite_note-211" id="mwB3Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB3U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB3Y">[</span>211<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB3c">]</span></span></a></sup> <span about="#mwt911" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwB3g" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"As of","href":"./Template:As_of"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2025"}},"i":0}}]}'>As of 2025</span><sup class="plainlinks noexcerpt noprint asof-tag update" style="display:none;" about="#mwt911"><a rel="mw:ExtLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Debian&amp;action=edit" class="external text" data-mw-original-href="//en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Debian&amp;action=edit"><span typeof="mw:Entity">[</span>update<span typeof="mw:Entity">]</span></a></sup><span typeof="mw:Nowiki" about="#mwt911"></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_potentially_dated_statements_from_2025" about="#mwt911"/><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:All_articles_containing_potentially_dated_statements" about="#mwt911" id="mwB3k"/>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DistroWatch" title="DistroWatch" id="mwB3o">DistroWatch</a> lists 141 active Debian derivatives.<sup about="#mwt920" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-212" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-212"}}'><a href="#cite_note-212" id="mwB3s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB3w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB30">[</span>212<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB34">]</span></span></a></sup> The Debian project provides its derivatives with guidelines for best practices and encourages derivatives to merge their work back into Debian.<sup about="#mwt923" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-213" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-213"}}'><a href="#cite_note-213" id="mwB38"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB4A"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB4E">[</span>213<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB4I">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt912" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHertzog2013429_214-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"group":"","name":"FOOTNOTEHertzog2013429"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-FOOTNOTEHertzog2013429-214"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"sfn","href":"./Template:Sfn"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Hertzog"},"2":{"wt":"2013"},"p":{"wt":"429"}},"i":0}}]}'><a href="#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHertzog2013429-214" id="mwB4M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB4Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB4U">[</span>214<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB4Y">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwB4c">Among the most notable of these distributions are <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ubuntu" title="Ubuntu" id="mwB4g">Ubuntu</a>, developed by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canonical_(company)" title="Canonical (company)" id="mwB4k">Canonical</a> and first released in 2004, which has surpassed Debian in popularity with desktop users;<sup about="#mwt928" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-215" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-215"}}'><a href="#cite_note-215" id="mwB4o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB4s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB4w">[</span>215<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB40">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knoppix" title="Knoppix" id="mwB44">Knoppix</a>, first released in the year 2000 and one of the first distributions optimized to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Live_USB" title="Live USB" id="mwB48">boot from external storage</a>; <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tails_(operating_system)" title="Tails (operating system)" id="mwB5A">Tails</a>, a distribution focused on privacy and security first released in 2009 and now part of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tor_Project" title="Tor Project" class="mw-redirect" id="mwB5E">Tor Project</a>,<sup about="#mwt931" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-216" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-216"}}'><a href="#cite_note-216" id="mwB5I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB5M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB5Q">[</span>216<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB5U">]</span></span></a></sup> known especially among journalists after <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_Snowden" title="Edward Snowden" id="mwB5Y">Edward Snowden</a> used it during his <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snowden_disclosures" title="Snowden disclosures" id="mwB5c">2013 disclosures</a>;<sup about="#mwt934" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-217" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-217"}}'><a href="#cite_note-217" id="mwB5g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB5k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB5o">[</span>217<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB5s">]</span></span></a></sup> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Devuan" title="Devuan" id="mwB5w">Devuan</a>, which gained attention in 2014 when it forked in disagreement over Debian's adoption of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Systemd" title="Systemd" id="mwB50">systemd</a> software suite, and has been mirroring Debian releases since 2017.<sup about="#mwt937" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-218" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-218"}}'><a href="#cite_note-218" id="mwB54"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB58"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB6A">[</span>218<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB6E">]</span></span></a></sup> The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linux_Mint#LMDE" title="Linux Mint" id="mwB6I">Linux Mint Debian Edition</a> has used Debian <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r886049734" about="#mwt924" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwB6M" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Mono/styles.css"},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"mono","href":"./Template:Mono"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"stable"}},"i":0}}]}'/><span class="monospaced" about="#mwt924" id="mwB6Q">stable</span> as the software source base since 2014.</p>

<section data-mw-section-id="55" id="mwB6U" aria-labelledby="Flavors"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Flavors">Flavors</h3><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Debian&amp;action=edit&amp;section=55" title="Edit section: Flavors"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<p id="mwB6Y"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Debian_Pure_Blend" title="Debian Pure Blend" id="mwB6c">Debian Pure Blends</a> are subsets of a Debian release configured out-of-the-box for users with particular skills and interests. For example, Debian Jr. is made for children, while <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Debian_Science" title="Debian Science" class="mw-redirect" id="mwB6g">Debian Science</a> is for researchers and scientists.<sup about="#mwt940" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-219" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-219"}}'><a href="#cite_note-219" id="mwB6k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB6o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB6s">[</span>219<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB6w">]</span></span></a></sup> The complete Debian distribution includes all available Debian Pure Blends.<sup about="#mwt943" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-220" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-220"}}'><a href="#cite_note-220" id="mwB60"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB64"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB68">[</span>220<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB7A">]</span></span></a></sup> "Debian Blend" (without "Pure") is a term for a Debian-based distribution that strives to become part of mainstream Debian, and have its extra features included in future releases.<sup about="#mwt946" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-221" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-221"}}'><a href="#cite_note-221" id="mwB7E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB7I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB7M">[</span>221<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB7Q">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="56" id="mwB7U" aria-labelledby="Debian_GNU/Hurd"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Debian_GNU/Hurd"><span id="Debian_GNU.2FHurd" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>Debian GNU/Hurd</h4><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Debian&amp;action=edit&amp;section=56" title="Edit section: Debian GNU/Hurd"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwB7Y"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Hurd-logo.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwB7c"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Hurd-logo.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Hurd-logo.svg/250px-Hurd-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Hurd-logo.svg/330px-Hurd-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="GNU Hurd logo" data-file-width="709" data-file-height="709" data-file-type="drawing" height="130" width="130" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.5" id="mwB7g"/></a><figcaption id="mwB7k">Logo of GNU Hurd</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-left" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwB7o"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Debian_GNU_HURD_XFCE_desktop_screenshot.png" class="mw-file-description" id="mwB7s"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Debian_GNU_HURD_XFCE_desktop_screenshot.png" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Debian_GNU_HURD_XFCE_desktop_screenshot.png/250px-Debian_GNU_HURD_XFCE_desktop_screenshot.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Debian_GNU_HURD_XFCE_desktop_screenshot.png/500px-Debian_GNU_HURD_XFCE_desktop_screenshot.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1280" data-file-height="768" data-file-type="bitmap" height="150" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwB7w"/></a><figcaption id="mwB70">Debian GNU/Hurd presenting the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xfce" title="Xfce" id="mwB74">Xfce</a> desktop environment running on <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/QEMU" title="QEMU" id="mwB78">QEMU</a></figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwB8A">Debian GNU/Hurd is a flavor based on the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU_Hurd" title="GNU Hurd" id="mwB8E">Hurd</a> kernel (which, in turn, runs on the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU_Mach" title="GNU Mach" id="mwB8I">GNU Mach</a> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microkernel" title="Microkernel" id="mwB8M">microkernel</a>), instead of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linux_kernel" title="Linux kernel" id="mwB8Q">Linux kernel</a>. Debian GNU/Hurd has been in development since 1998,<sup about="#mwt949" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-222" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-222"}}'><a href="#cite_note-222" id="mwB8U"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB8Y"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB8c">[</span>222<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB8g">]</span></span></a></sup> and made a formal release in May 2013, with 78% of the software packaged for Debian ported to the GNU Hurd.<sup about="#mwt952" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-223" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-223"}}'><a href="#cite_note-223" id="mwB8k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB8o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB8s">[</span>223<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB8w">]</span></span></a></sup> Hurd is not yet an official Debian release, and is maintained and developed as an unofficial port. Debian GNU/Hurd is distributed as an installer CD (running the official Debian installer) or ready-to-run <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virtual_disk_image" title="Virtual disk image" class="mw-redirect" id="mwB80">virtual disk image</a> (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Live_CD" title="Live CD" id="mwB84">Live CD</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Live_USB" title="Live USB" id="mwB88">Live USB</a>). The CD uses the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IA-32" title="IA-32" id="mwB9A">IA-32</a> architecture, making it compatible with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IA-32" title="IA-32" id="mwB9E">IA-32</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X86-64" title="X86-64" id="mwB9I">x86-64</a> PCs.<sup about="#mwt955" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-224" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-224"}}'><a href="#cite_note-224" id="mwB9M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB9Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB9U">[</span>224<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB9Y">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="57" id="mwB9c" aria-labelledby="Debian_GNU/kFreeBSD"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Debian_GNU/kFreeBSD"><span id="Debian_GNU.2FkFreeBSD" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>Debian GNU/kFreeBSD</h4><span class="mw-editsection"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a href="/w/index.php?title=Debian&amp;action=edit&amp;section=57" title="Edit section: Debian GNU/kFreeBSD"><span>edit</span></a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwB9g"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Horned_logo.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwB9k"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Horned_logo.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Horned_logo.svg/250px-Horned_logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Horned_logo.svg/330px-Horned_logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="Debian GNU/kFreeBSD logo" data-file-width="600" data-file-height="600" data-file-type="drawing" height="130" width="130" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 0.5" id="mwB9o"/></a><figcaption id="mwB9s">Logo of Debian GNU/kFreeBSD</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwB9w">Debian GNU/kFreeBSD is a discontinued Debian flavor.<sup about="#mwt959" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-225" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-225"}}'><a href="#cite_note-225" id="mwB90"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB94"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB98">[</span>225<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB-A">]</span></span></a></sup> It used the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FreeBSD" title="FreeBSD" id="mwB-E">FreeBSD</a> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kernel_(operating_system)" title="Kernel (operating system)" id="mwB-I">kernel</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU" title="GNU" id="mwB-M">GNU</a> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_space" title="User space" class="mw-redirect" id="mwB-Q">userland</a>. The majority of software in Debian GNU/kFreeBSD was built from the same sources as Debian, with some kernel packages from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FreeBSD" title="FreeBSD" id="mwB-U">FreeBSD</a>. The <i id="mwB-Y">k</i> in <i id="mwB-c">kFreeBSD</i> is an abbreviation for <i id="mwB-g">kernel</i>, which refers to the FreeBSD kernel. Before discontinuing the project, Debian maintained i386 and amd64 ports. The last version of Debian kFreeBSD was Debian 8 "Jessie" RC3. Debian GNU/kFreeBSD was created in 2002.<sup about="#mwt962" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-226" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-226"}}'><a href="#cite_note-226" id="mwB-k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB-o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB-s">[</span>226<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB-w">]</span></span></a></sup> It was included in Debian 6.0 "Squeeze" as a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technology_preview" title="Technology preview" class="mw-redirect" id="mwB-0">technology preview</a>, and in Debian 7 "Wheezy" as an official port.<link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:All_articles_with_unsourced_statements" about="#mwt956" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwB-4" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Citation needed","href":"./Template:Citation_needed"},"params":{"date":{"wt":"August 2024"}},"i":0}}]}'/><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_with_unsourced_statements_from_August_2024" about="#mwt956"/><sup class="noprint Inline-Template Template-Fact" style="white-space:nowrap;" about="#mwt956" id="mwB-8"><span typeof="mw:Entity">[</span><i><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed" title="Wikipedia:Citation needed"><span title="This claim needs references to reliable sources. (August 2024)">citation needed</span></a></i><span typeof="mw:Entity">]</span></sup> Debian GNU/kFreeBSD was discontinued as an officially supported platform as of Debian 8. Debian developers cited <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_Sound_System" title="Open Sound System" id="mwB_A">OSS</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PF_(firewall)" title="PF (firewall)" id="mwB_E">pf</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FreeBSD_jail" title="FreeBSD jail" id="mwB_I">jails</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Network_Driver_Interface_Specification" title="Network Driver Interface Specification" id="mwB_M">NDIS</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ZFS" title="ZFS" id="mwB_Q">ZFS</a> as reasons for being interested in the FreeBSD kernel.<sup about="#mwt965" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-227" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-227"}}'><a href="#cite_note-227" id="mwB_U"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB_Y"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB_c">[</span>227<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB_g">]</span></span></a></sup> It has not been officially updated since Debian 8.<sup about="#mwt968" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-228" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-228"}}'><a href="#cite_note-228" id="mwB_k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB_o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB_s">[</span>228<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB_w">]</span></span></a></sup> However, starting in July 2019, the operating system continued to be maintained unofficially. As of July 2023, the development of Debian GNU/kFreeBSD has officially terminated due to the lack of interest and developers.<sup about="#mwt971" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-229" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-229"}}'><a href="#cite_note-229" id="mwB_0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwB_4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwB_8">[</span>229<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCAA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

